    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 241122\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n422 AM MST Sat Oct 24 2026\n\n.UPDATED...Updated Aviation Discussion...\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Below normal temperatures will continue through the weekend \n  before readings warm back closer to the seasonal average for the\n  bulk of next week.\n\n- Aside from a few showers mainly over the mountains of Arizona \n  today, dry and tranquil weather conditions will grace the region\n  through the middle of next week.\n\n- A return to inclement weather with increased rainfall chances \n  is possible towards the end of next week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \n\nAnalysis early this morning showed a recently closed off low\npressure system across the Southwest with a prominent vorticity \ncenter rounding the base of the low and moving east across SoCal \nwith some light showers in Imperial County and southern Yuma\nCounty. This low will slowly track across AZ today through Sunday\nand exit through the Four Corners by Monday. As the cold-core\npushes into AZ this afternoon it will be enough, combined with \nsurface heating, to generate scattered showers and isolated storms\nacross the northern AZ high terrain, including portions of Gila\nCounty. A few showers may also develop across Maricopa and Pinal \nCounties this afternoon as the vorticity center passes overhead, \nbut with low mixing ratios around 4-6 g/kg any showers will \nlikely be inconsequential and not lead to much in the way of \nrainfall. A brief thunderstorm cannot be completely ruled out as \nmid-level lapse rates increase to around 7 C/km, with chances\nbetter in the foothills north and northeast of Phoenix. Maybe \neven a cold-air funnel. H5 heights around 565-572 dam this \nweekend will keep temperatures 3-6 degrees below normal, with \nlower desert highs in the upper 70s to middle 80s and lows in the \n50s to lower 60s. With light winds and the below normal\ntemperatures it should be a comfortable Fall weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \n\nGradually warming temperatures and overall tranquil weather is\nexpected through at least the first half of the next workweek with\nquasi-zonal flow in the wake of the weekend low. An increase in H5\nheights up to 579-582 dam by Monday will support the gradual\nwarming of temperatures, with afternoon highs reaching the middle\n80s and lows in the upper 50s to lower 60s across the lower\ndeserts, which is right around normal for the time of year. Dry \nmostly clear skies will prevail under this pattern as well. Model \nuncertainty grows later next week as there are hints at some \nmoisture return into the region from the southeast, then a \npotential atmospheric river approaching by next weekend. Either, \nor both of these moisture sources may eventually help to bring a \nreturn to rain chances at some point before the end of the month, \nbut it is too early to have a good idea on timing and/or placement\nfor any weather feature this far in advance. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1120Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \n\nNo major aviation weather concerns are expected throughout the \nTAF period. Can expect some late morning and afternoon CU aoa \n7kft and maybe some isolated showers, but they should pose no \nreal impact and confidence is too low to include in the TAFs. The\noverall wind pattern will exhibit the typical diurnal tendencies \nwith overall speeds aob 7 kts along with periods of light \nvariability to even calm conditions. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo aviation weather concerns are expected throughout the TAF\nperiod. Winds will generally fluctuate between the west to north\nwith overall speeds aob 7 kts along with periods of light\nvariability. Other than some SCT to BKN cloud decks around 10 kft\nearly this morning, mostly clear skies are expected.\n\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nA low pressure system will linger across the region this weekend \nbringing light shower chances mainly to mountain areas today, but\nno wetting rainfall is expected. Minimum humidity levels will \nfall into a 25-50% range this afternoon and then dry heading into\nthe start of the workweek, with minimum humidity falling closer to\n15-35%. Overnight recoveries will remain good at 45-70% across the\narea. Overall quiet weather conditions are forecast Sunday through\nat least the first half of next week, with temperatures \nrebounding back into the normal range by Monday, and with light \nwinds each day favoring typical diurnal tendencies. A shift in the\nweather pattern late next week and into next week may bring about\ncooler, wetter, and breezier conditions.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Benedict\nLONG TERM...18/Benedict\nAVIATION...Lojero/Kuhlman\nFIRE WEATHER...Benedict/Kuhlman\n",
